{"author":{"id":"7674679dac9170669f752d932f8b5955b1d55717359e13b1c1e687003c7bb99f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794173325,"nonce":"CzE+7k1deddWmHAZo80yvGYCjV8=","message":"Built as designed, with one point the design left open:\n\n- The dry run applies the whole derived schema in memory (every change the import would write, archived values included, via the import's own dry run, `schema.Compile(schema.Apply(current, changes))`), compiles the mapping from it, and the engine checks against it through `Options.Schema` and `cache.RepoCache.CheckerFor`.\n- Identities are not handled: a key whose value needs a Jira user the dry run did not create stays pending, as before. `doc/design/jira.md`, \"The report\", says so; the schema half moves under a new heading in the same aspect, \"A dry run plans against the derived schema\".\n- The fake gains `Server.AddStatus` (a status added to a type's workflow) for the test.","files":null}]}